# `.drop` IS A STATEMENT, NOT A VALUE -- also where a line's value is used. A
# block's value line and an `out` function's last line (its implied return) are
# statements whose value IS used; `x.drop` there yields nothing. It was refused
# only off a statement, so both reached the C as a member access on the value
# ("request for member 'drop' in something not a structure or union"). A
# `.drop` on a line whose value nothing uses is legal (drop_statement_positions).
widen: function {x: u8} out i64 is {
    x.drop
}

main: function is {
    n: 3
    r: {
        n.drop
    }
    print "\{r} \{widen x: 1}"
}
